 arromdee@cs.jhu.edu (Ken Arromdee) writes:
> xanthian@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG (Kent Paul Dolan) writes:

>> That one doesn't irritate me nearly as much as having
>> monsters who blunder around the room swatting at
>> shadows when they are trying to hit my invisible
>> character somehow develop infallible aim as soon as
>> they change from swinging things to throwing things.

> But they don't. You don't get a _message_ saying
> "the arrow hits the place where the orc-captain
> thinks you are", but it happens. Monsters will
> even fire missiles when out of line with you, if
> they _think_ they are in line with you....

OK, I guess that makes sense algorithmically, but not
for game play.  When _I_ throw a throwing weapon or
fire a missile weapon at nowhere in particular, the
weapon or missile falls to the floor, and I see it
there.  The same should happen for monsters aiming
where I'm not.

Moreover, since _I_ see _them_, and thus what they
are doing, I should indeed get a message saying
"the orc throws the dagger where he thinks he hears
you, but misses [you incredibly clever adventurer
who managed to make yourself permanently invisible]";
silence except when the weapon hits me or hits the
square where I am but misses due to armor class gives
a very different impression, the one I mentioned
above, rather than an impession of what the adventurer
would really see.

I think it should be fixed, but then I don't have to
do the work, so it's easy for me to say.

